# Production of {{ title }}

The API runs on a server of one's own, `{{ vps_host }}`, in a podman-compose stack
(`/opt/{{ name }}`), behind Caddy which serves `https://{{ api_domain }}`. Everything that runs on
the server is versioned in `config/vps/`; what is run from the workstation is in `scripts/ops/`.
The server's descriptor (address, branch, public URLs) is `config/vps/project.json`.

| File | Role |
|---|---|
| `config/vps/compose.yaml` | Production stack: PostgreSQL and the backend (gunicorn), read-only |
| `config/vps/Caddyfile` | Automatic TLS and proxy to `127.0.0.1:8000` |
| `config/vps/deploy-remote.sh` | Switch as root: one image per commit, migrations, automatic rollback |
| `config/vps/backup.sh` | Daily and pre-switch backup; restic copy off the server |
| `config/vps/restore-test.sh` | Restore of the latest backup every week, timed |
| `config/vps/heal.sh` | Restart of the containers whose probe fails, every 5 minutes |
| `config/vps/setup-deploy-user.sh` | The `deploy` user, to stop deploying as root |
| `scripts/ops/deploy-backend.sh` | Deployment from the workstation (`--dry-run`, `--rollback`) |
| `scripts/ops/smoke-test.sh` | Smoke test from outside, after each deployment |

## Prepare the server (once)

On a recent Debian or Ubuntu, as root:

```sh
apt install podman caddy git curl
# podman-compose through uv, never pip ([UV-PODMAN]):
curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh && uv tool install podman-compose
ln -s /root/.local/bin/podman-compose /usr/local/bin/podman-compose
apt install restic   # optional: copy of the backups off the server
```

Then, in `/opt/{{ name }}` (created when needed), the secrets, never versioned ([SECRETS]):

- `/opt/{{ name }}/.env`: `POSTGRES_PASSWORD=<long and random>` (`openssl rand -hex 24`), mode 600;
- `/opt/{{ name }}/backend/.env`: from `backend/.env.example`, with `DJANGO_DEBUG=False`, a random
  `DJANGO_SECRET_KEY`, `DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS={{ api_domain }}` and
  `DJANGO_CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS=https://{{ api_domain }}`, mode 600;
- `/root/.restic-env` (optional): `RESTIC_REPOSITORY`, `RESTIC_PASSWORD` and, for an S3 bucket,
  `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` and `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`, mode 600.

The DNS of `{{ api_domain }}` points to the server before the first deployment: Caddy needs it to
get the certificate.

## Deploy

```sh
scripts/ops/deploy-backend.sh --dry-run   # image and checks, no switch
scripts/ops/deploy-backend.sh             # switch
```

The script refuses a commit whose CI is not green (`SKIP_CI=1` in an emergency) and a local
branch that differs from `origin`. On the server, the switch:

1. receives the code as a git bundle, builds the image `{{ name | replace('-', '_') }}_backend:<sha>`
   and checks that it carries that commit;
2. validates the compose stack, the Caddyfile, the scripts and `manage.py check --deploy`;
3. installs scripts, systemd units and Caddyfile, then backs up the database;
4. applies the migrations (the backend only stops when there are some);
5. switches the backend and waits for `/api/health` to return the new version; otherwise,
   automatic rollback to the previous one;
6. checks the end-to-end probe, through `https://{{ api_domain }}`.

The first deployment runs as root. Then create the `deploy` user
(`bash config/vps/setup-deploy-user.sh "<public key>"` on the server), change `host` in
`project.json` to `deploy@…`, and remove root's SSH key.

## Roll back

```sh
scripts/ops/deploy-backend.sh --rollback
```

Puts the previous image back in service. Migrations are not undone: if the version to undo
migrated the schema, also restore the backup taken before the switch ([REVERSIBLE-DEPLOYMENT]):

```sh
podman exec -i {{ name }}-db pg_restore -U {{ name | replace('-', '_') }} -d {{ name | replace('-', '_') }} --clean --if-exists < /root/backups/{{ name }}-pre-deploy-<date>.dump
```

## Backups

- Daily at 3:15 (`{{ name }}-backup.timer`), 14 kept; one before each switch, 10 kept; in
  `/root/backups`. A restic copy off the server when `/root/.restic-env` exists.
- Restore tested every Sunday (`{{ name }}-restore-test.timer`) in a throwaway PostgreSQL:
  `journalctl -u {{ name }}-restore-test` gives the number of tables and the duration
  ([TESTED-BACKUPS]).
- State of the timers: `systemctl list-timers '{{ name }}-*'`.

## Diagnose

```sh
podman ps                                  # containers and the state of their probes
podman logs --tail 100 {{ name }}-backend   # API logs
ls /var/log/{{ name }}-deploy/              # log of each switch
cat /opt/{{ name }}/deploy-history.log      # deployed versions
```
